      "text": "To find the positive value of \\( t \\) for which \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we start by using the definition of the magnitude of a complex number. The magnitude of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\).\n\nHere, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\), so the magnitude is:\n\\[\n|-4 + ti| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2}\n\\]\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\). Therefore, we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\nNext, we solve for \\( t^2 \\) by isolating it on one side of the equation:\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\nTaking the positive square root of both sides (since we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\)):\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
