      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the positive value of \\( t \\) such that \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\).\n\nThe magnitude of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\). Here, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\), so we have \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\). Therefore, the magnitude is:\n\n\\[\n|-4 + ti| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\n\nNext, we isolate \\( t^2 \\) by subtracting 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\), we take the positive square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nt = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
